WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.910 --> 00:00:05.860
We are now ready to present the algorithm
for solving the decoding problem that

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:10.300
was developed by Andrew Viterbi for decoding.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:13.220
To implement the dynamic programming for
decoding problem,

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:20.220
we introduce the variable score_(k,i), which
corresponds to the maximum product weight

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:25.240
among all paths from source to node_(k,i),

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:31.210
or the node corresponding to state k and
located in the ith column of the graph.

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:39.450
Let's consider all predecessors of this
node corresponding to all possible states.

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:44.770
And obviously, the score at the node_(k,i)

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:50.360
equals the maximums through all
possible states l of scores

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:55.400
in the previous column multiplied
by the weight of the edge

00:00:55.400 --> 00:01:01.780
from the node in the previous column,
node l, to the node k in the column i,

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:06.010
which is, in other words,
the maximum through all state

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:11.460
scores in the previous column
multiplied by the weight of

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:17.410
the edge (l,k,i-1).

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:21.808
So the recurrence for
Viterbi Algorithm with be

00:01:21.808 --> 00:01:25.839
score_(k,i) equals the maximum through all states:

00:01:25.839 --> 00:01:31.330
score_(l,i-1) * weight_(l,k,i-1).

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:35.950
The initialization, of course,
is score_source = 1.

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:40.960
And the maximum product weight
over all paths from source to sink

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:47.220
is computed at the node sink, which
the score of sink will be the maximum

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:54.380
through all states of the scores
in the last column of the Manhattan.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:57.550
Now, lets estimate the running
time of the Viterbi Algorithm

00:01:57.550 --> 00:02:01.950
in the case when HMM has
forbidden transition.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:05.730
Forbidden transition means
that the probability of switching

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:07.400
for a given pair of states is zero.

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:12.520
For example, in this HMM diagram,
there are four states,

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:15.800
but as you see,
there are no edges connecting some states.

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:19.340
They correspond to forbidden
transitions in the HMM.

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:26.910
You notice that the number of edges in
this case in the Viterbi's Manhattan is smaller

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:32.030
than for the case of the full HMM diagram,
when all transitions are allowed.

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:36.770
And in this case, the running time is,
of course, number of edges

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:42.480
in the HMM diagram multiplied by
the number of emitted symbols n.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:46.370
You, of course,
notice that, if in the past we were

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:50.530
maximizing the sum of weights of
edges in the paths in the graph,

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:55.980
now we maximize the product of edges for
the paths in the graph.

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:59.630
And when we maximize the product,
there is a danger of overflow.

00:02:59.630 --> 00:03:04.540
Therefore, bioinformaticians prefer
to work with logarithms of scores.

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:08.834
So if you transform the currency for

00:03:08.834 --> 00:03:12.740
the score_(k,i) by taking its logarithm,

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:16.950
then the currency corresponding
to the product of weights

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:22.460
is transformed into the currency
corresponding to the sum of weights,

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:27.030
and this transformation substitutes
weight of edges by their logarithms

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:32.060
and transforms product of weights
into the familiar sum of weights,

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:37.000
so there is no difference between
computing an optimal solution

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:42.080
in the decoding problem and
the traditional longest path in the graph,

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:44.605
something that we studied before.

00:03:44.605 --> 00:03:49.640
Note that we have already learned
how to compute the probability

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:53.900
of the hidden path pi, and
it turned out to be easy.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:57.655
It amounted simply to multiplication
of transition probability,

00:03:57.655 --> 00:04:04.270
and our intuition tells us that it will probably
be equally easy to compute the probability

00:04:04.270 --> 00:04:09.370
of the emitted sequence x. Let's see
whether our intuition is correct.

00:04:09.370 --> 00:04:14.090
So, when we compute Pr(pi),
it is simply multiplication of transition

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:20.090
probabilities, but when we compute Pr(x),
it's the sum

00:04:20.090 --> 00:04:25.680
through all possible paths pi
of Pr(x,pi),

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:29.330
and it is not clear how
to compute this sum.

00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:37.240
Note that Pr(x) is the sum through
all possible hidden paths pi

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:42.010
of the product weight of pi.

00:04:42.010 --> 00:04:47.290
And compared with the score at sink
in our Viterbi Manhattan,

00:04:47.290 --> 00:04:54.140
which is the maximum through all possible
paths pi of the product weight of pi.

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:58.050
These two expressions
look extremely similar.

00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:02.560
Lets try to see whether we can
explore this similarity to come up

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:07.510
with an algorithm for computing
the probability of the emitted sequence x.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:13.090
So to find out what is the most
likely outcome of an HMM,

00:05:13.090 --> 00:05:17.590
we must first need to solve the following
outcome likelihood problem:

00:05:17.590 --> 00:05:22.090
Find the probability that
an HMM emits a given string.

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:25.940
The input is a string x emitted, by an HMM,

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:31.690
and the output is the probability
that the HMM emits this string.

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:38.780
And since you already saw the similarity
between computing probability of x and

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:44.160
the Viterbi algorithm, let's try to
figure out whether we can solve

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:50.040
the outcome likelihood problem by changing
a single symbol in the Viterbi recurrence.

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:56.410
So, let's try to modify
the Viterbi algorithm and

00:05:56.410 --> 00:05:59.980
introduce a variable forward_(k,i),

00:05:59.980 --> 00:06:05.614
which is the total product weight
of all paths to the node (k,i).

00:06:05.614 --> 00:06:11.150
Here are all predecessor of node k,
and therefore,

00:06:11.150 --> 00:06:19.532
forward_(k,i) is equal to the sum through all
possible states of the values

00:06:20.590 --> 00:06:23.910
forward_(l,i-1) in the previous column

00:06:23.910 --> 00:06:29.560
multiplied by the weight of the edge
from the previous column to node k,

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:33.240
which is simply the sum
through all state l:

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:37.816
forward_(l,i-1) * weight_(l,k,i-1).

00:06:37.816 --> 00:06:41.661
forward_(l,i-1) * weight_(l,k,i-1).

00:06:41.661 --> 00:06:46.482
And, the only difference between this

00:06:46.482 --> 00:06:51.596
recurrence for forward_(k,i) and score_(k,i)

00:06:51.596 --> 00:06:56.430
is the change of maximum into sum.

00:06:56.430 --> 00:07:00.236
Now, that's all interesting,
but you may have the question:

00:07:00.236 --> 00:07:04.022
What does it all have to do with
biological applications?

00:07:04.022 --> 00:07:07.516
And, in the next section,
we will start looking

00:07:07.516 --> 00:07:11.869
at the application of HMMs
to finding protein families.

